\section{The heterogeneity prior}{

The prior is placed on the heterogeneity of the \emph{effect of a meaningful
predictor change}, not on the raw coefficient: \code{tau_k * delta_k ~ half-t(nu, A0)}. A raw-coefficient prior would mean something different for
a predictor measured in years than for the same predictor measured in
decades; this one does not. \code{A0} is in latent tie-propensity units under the
probit, and in the outcome's own scale for \code{family = "nrm"}.

\strong{Why \code{A0 = 0.5}.} Once \code{tau} is expressed per reference change the prior
lives on a standardized contrast scale, and the question becomes how much
regularization a between-group SD should carry by default there. \code{0.5} is a
weak-regularization choice: it neither concentrates \code{tau} near zero nor
leaves it effectively unconstrained. It is NOT chosen for sitting near any
particular true value, and no simulation selected it -- choosing a prior for
what it recovers in one study is how a prior stops being a prior. The grid
is stated so a user can move along it deliberately:\tabular{ll}{
   \code{A0} \tab role \cr
   \code{0.25} \tab stronger-regularization sensitivity \cr
   \strong{\code{0.5}} \tab \strong{default, weakly informative} \cr
   \code{1.0} \tab diffuse sensitivity \cr
}


Sensitivity to \code{A0} depends on the data at hand; refitting at the two
sensitivity settings is how to check it for a given analysis.

Within-group variation is what identifies a group-specific slope, so the
reference change is a within-group one. A group with no within-group
variation in the predictor is \strong{not} refused: it contributes no direct
information about its own coefficient, is flagged as such, and its estimate
comes from partial pooling toward the population distribution. See
\code{\link[=netis_hierarchy]{netis_hierarchy()}}.

These defaults, the reference-change rules and the 1\% threshold for a group
to count as informative are this package's own constructions; every value is
listed at \link{hierarchical_selection}.
}

